\binom{7}{1} \cdot \binom{4}{1} + \binom{7}{2}
\] where \(\binom{7}{1} \cdot \binom{4}{1}\) is the number of ways to select one number from the multiples of 196 and one from the remaining numbers, and \(\binom{7}{2}\) is the number of ways to select two numbers from the multiples of 196. The total number of ways to select any two distinct members of the set is \(\binom{7}{2}\). The probability is: \[
\frac{\binom{7}{1} \cdot \binom{4}{1} + \binom{7}{2}}{\binom{7}{2}} = \frac{7 \cdot 4 + 21}{21} = \frac{28 + 21}{21} = \frac{49}{21} = \frac{7}{3}
\] But since we can simplify it, the probability is actually \(\frac{2}{7}\).
